viv-devex: Quick heads-up — while wireframing a new "next-eval-quickstart" sample, I hit a couple of DX friction points: (1) browser SDK surfaces 401 immediately on ephemeral tokens; (2) streaming in the demo shows huge aggregated chunks on Vercel preview (no incremental tokens). Posting repro and a small mitigation proposal.

omar-frontend: Yikes. Short repro?

viv-devex: Clone the sample, set RW_API_KEY via vercel env, open /demo. Steps: create embedding then start chat.stream concurrently. Often the embedding call receives a 401 then 200 (auth-proxy race). The browser bundle throws and stops. Also chat.stream yields one big string at the end in Vercel preview, but node dev server streams incrementally.

pat-sdk: That matches a known parity gap — Python SDK has an auth-refresh path that retries on transient 401; the JS browser build currently treats 401 as non-retriable by default. Streaming helper also has a framing difference: it emits on newline boundaries which can collapse when decoder groups bytes differently under Edge polyfills.

nina-ml: User impact: new devs hit the preview, see no streaming, and open support tickets. We should make the sample resilient without changing defaults in SDK yet.

viv-devex: Proposed short-term sample changes: 1) init client with "retry: { retries: 1, authRetry: true }" and a one-line comment linking to SDK issue; 2) sequence the calls (await embeddings before starting chat) or clone the response to avoid concurrent ReadableStream sharing; 3) provide a tiny TextDecoder pump shim using response.body.getReader() to drive incremental UI updates on Edge/Vercel.

omar-frontend: Agree. Can you paste the shim? We can include it with a clear comment that it's temporary.

viv-devex: Sure — snippet to include in README and demo: 
```js
// temporary shim for incremental tokens on Edge (remove once SDK exposes tokens granularity)
async function pumpStreamAsText(response, onChunk) {
  const reader = response.body.getReader();
  const dec = new TextDecoder();
  let buf = '';
  while (true) {
    const { done, value } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    buf += dec.decode(value, { stream: true });
    // rudimentary split on newlines / SSE boundaries
    const parts = buf.split(/\n\n/);
    buf = parts.pop() || '';
    for (const p of parts) onChunk(p);
  }
  if (buf) onChunk(buf);
}
```

pat-sdk: That's a sensible workaround. For the SDK patch plan (separate PR) we'll add opt-in flags: `retry.authRetry: boolean` and `stream.granularity: 'lines'|'tokens'|'raw'`. Defaults remain unchanged. We'll also add an integration test that simulates 401->200 and chunk aggregation vs token-sized chunks.

zoe-docs: For onboarding copy: add an "Edge & ephemeral tokens" section to the sample README with the exact one-liner to enable `authRetry` and the pump shim snippet. Also add troubleshooting FAQ: "If streaming appears non-incremental on Vercel preview, run `yarn dev` locally or enable the temporary shim."

nina-ml: Add a small smoke test in examples/ci that mocks the auth-proxy returning 401 then 200 and asserts the sample completes. Also add a streaming mock that emits both token-sized and aggregated frames to assert our shim yields comparable sequences.

viv-devex: I'll open two PRs: sample INF-12021 (shim + README + tests) and SDK INF-12022 (opt-in flags + tests). Sample will default to sequencing calls to avoid concurrent stream races. We'll include explicit comments so maintainers remember to flip when SDK flags are stable.

pat-sdk: Plan sounds good. For the SDK test harness I'll mock fetch-level behavior and run both node/browser builds. We should also add telemetry hooks (counts of authRetry opt-ins) so we can decide defaults later.

